  "Question": "\\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are independent, and \\(P(B)=1/3\\). What is \\(P(B\\mid A)\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "1/3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "When \\(P(A)>0\\), independence means the conditional probability equals the event's own probability, because\n\\[\nP(B\\mid A)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}=\\frac{P(A)P(B)}{P(A)}=\\frac13.\n\\]\nThe question does not state \\(P(A)>0\\). For example, take \\(A=\\varnothing\\) and an event \\(B\\) with probability \\(1/3\\). These events are independent since \\(P(A\\cap B)=0=P(A)P(B)\\), but the defining conditional-probability ratio is \\(0/0\\), which is undefined.\n\nThus the answer is \\(1/3\\) if \\(P(A)>0\\), and undefined if \\(P(A)=0\\). The key requires the missing positive-probability condition.",
